  "Number": 9,
  "Question": "The sum of the real values of \\(x\\) satisfying \\(\\lvert x+2\\rvert=2\\lvert x-2\\rvert\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac13\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac23\\)",
    "C": "\\(6\\)",
    "D": "\\(6\\dfrac13\\)",
    "E": "\\(6\\dfrac23\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Because \\(2\\) is positive, \\(2|x-2|=|2(x-2)|\\). Two real numbers have the same absolute value exactly when they are equal or are opposites. Thus the original equation is equivalent to the following two possibilities:\n\\[\nx+2=2(x-2)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=6,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nx+2=-2(x-2)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=\\frac23.\n\\]\nThe equal-or-opposite rule includes zero and covers every real possibility. Each branch also implies the original equality, so both solutions are valid and there are no others. Their sum is\n\\[\n6+\\frac23=\\frac{20}{3}=6\\tfrac23,\n\\]\nchoice E.",
